From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Josep Maria Blasco <josep.maria.blasco@epbcn.com>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 11:46:41 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 5/5] WebAssembly port: browser runtime for web pages

wasm/build-web.sh relinks the build into a modular browser runtime
(createOoRexx) with a small page API, wasm/web/ooRexx.js:
OoRexx.load() and run({args, files, cwd, env, stdin, onOut, onErr,
onInput}) -> {write, close, stop, done}.

- one Web Worker per run hosts the Emscripten runtime
  (wasm/web/ooRexx-worker.js), the interpreter's pthreads are nested
  workers; stop = terminate the worker; the compiled module and rexx.data
  are fetched once;
- interactive stdin (wasm/web-stdin.cpp, --wrap=read): fd 0 is served on
  the calling pthread from a ring in shared memory with a futex wait;
  LINES()/CHARS() work on it;
- raw stdout/stderr, input files and cwd, files collected after the run;
- onOut/onErr say what each piece is ('plain', 'trace', 'error' message)
  and onInput whether the read is interactive trace's: a weak hook in
  Activity.cpp (orx_wasm_mark) marks it per thread, and each write to
  fd 1/2 (--wrap=write, one at a time) or read of fd 0 publishes the
  thread's mark for the page, so threads writing at once keep their kinds;
- the portable extension classes preloaded in /usr/lib/ooRexx;
- Atomics.waitAsync hidden in the runtime's workers: WebKit sometimes loses
  its wakeups, and a run whose system call proxied to the runtime's thread
  is never answered hangs (~5-10% of runs); Emscripten then uses postMessage;
- Date.now() with the resolution of performance.now(), so that MEMFS stamps
  files with the interpreter's (sub-millisecond) clock;
- .RexxInfo~libraryPath is /usr/lib/ooRexx/, where the classes are
  preloaded; .RexxInfo~executable stays .nil (the executable, rexx.wasm,
  comes from a URL, not from the program's file system);
- test page wasm/tests/web-api.html, and wasm/tests/web-api.py: assertions
  over the API in headless Chromium.
